<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    ============================================================
    ic_photo — photo/image glyph (attach menu, media bubble badge)
    ===
    Referenced by:
      - bottom_sheet_attach.xml (option_photo leading icon)
      - future: image message bubble type badge (Phase 7)
      - future: full-screen image viewer action bar (Phase 7)

    Constraints this file honors:

    1. MONOCHROME TEMPLATE.
       Single fill color (#FFFFFFFF), tinted at the consumer
       level (app:tint="?attr/colorOnSurface" in
       bottom_sheet_attach.xml, resolving to md_on_surface_light
       in light mode and md_on_surface_dark in dark mode). Do
       NOT set a brand color here — the fill is a template.

    2. 24dp VIEWPORT.
       Matches every other chrome icon in the app. The bottom
       sheet's option tiles render it at 24dp (its native size);
       future media-bubble badges may render it at 16dp or
       20dp — the vector scales losslessly.

    3. STANDARD MATERIAL "IMAGE" GLYPH.
       This is the canonical Material Design "image" icon — a
       filled rounded rectangle with a mountain landscape cut
       out of the bottom portion. It is NOT a custom design.
       Photo is a universal affordance and users recognize
       this exact glyph from every photo/gallery app in the
       world; any deviation reads as a rendering bug rather
       than a design choice.

    4. FILLED RECTANGLE, CUT-OUT LANDSCAPE.
       The icon is a solid rectangle with the "landscape"
       region punched out (transparent). Rendered on the
       surface-variant background of the attach option tile,
       this produces a dark rounded rectangle (the tint color)
       with a lighter landscape shape showing through. This
       is the same visual as every other Material app's
       "photo" icon.

       Do NOT switch to the "image_outlined" variant
       (border-only rectangle with solid landscape inside)
       — that variant is appropriate for toolbar actions
       where the icon must not compete with the toolbar's
       other content. In the attach sheet, a filled icon
       has the right visual weight to indicate "tap to
       send a photo."

    5. FILL TYPE = evenOdd.
       The mountain landscape is a "hole" in the filled
       rectangle. The canonical Material path expresses this
       via opposite winding directions between the two
       subpaths (rectangle CW, mountain CCW), which renders
       correctly under nonZero fill. However, to be robust
       against any tooling that might normalize winding
       directions during a future refactor, fillType is
       set to "evenOdd" explicitly. This guarantees the
       cutout regardless of winding and produces the same
       visual as the canonical Google asset. Do NOT remove
       this attribute.

    6. SINGLE PATH WITH TWO CONTOURS.
       The path data contains the outer rectangle contour
       (first M...z block) and the mountain contour
       (second M...z block) in a single <path> element.
       This is the standard Material construction — a
       separate <path> for the mountain would produce a
       filled mountain on top of the rectangle (via the
       default nonZero for that path alone), which is the
       opposite of what we want. Keep both contours in
       the single path element.

    7. NO androidx.AutoMirrored.
       A landscape photo icon is not directional.
       Mirroring under RTL produces no visible change.
       Do not add auto-mirroring.

    Rendering result at 24dp:
      A rounded-rectangle photo frame with a mountain
      landscape cut out of it — immediately readable as
      "photo / image" for any user of any photo app.
    ============================================================
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="24dp"
    android:height="24dp"
    android:viewportWidth="24"
    android:viewportHeight="24">

    <!--
        Canonical Material Design "image" path.

        Contour 1 — outer rectangle (rounded corners):
          M 21,19              start at bottom-right
          V 5                  up the right edge
          c0,-1.1 -0.9,-2 -2,-2  top-right corner curve
          H 5                  left across the top
          c-1.1,0 -2,0.9 -2,2  top-left corner curve
          v 14                 down the left edge
          c0,1.1 0.9,2 2,2     bottom-left corner curve
          h 14                 right across the bottom
          c1.1,0 2,-0.9 2,-2   bottom-right corner curve
          z                    close (back to start)

        Contour 2 — mountain landscape (cutout):
          M 8.5,13.5           left mountain peak
          l 2.5,3.01           slope down-right
          L 14.5,12            right mountain peak (higher)
          l 4.5,6              slope down-right to canvas
          H 5                  horizontal line back to left
          l 3.5,-4.5           slope up-right, close mountain
          z                    close

        Rendered together under evenOdd, the rectangle is
        filled (the tint color) and the mountain region is
        cut out (transparent), producing the standard "photo
        with landscape" visual.
    -->
    <path
        android:fillColor="#FFFFFFFF"
        android:fillType="evenOdd"
        android:pathData="M21,19V5c0,-1.1 -0.9,-2 -2,-2H5c-1.1,0 -2,0.9 -2,2v14c0,1.1 0.9,2 2,2h14c1.1,0 2,-0.9 2,-2zM8.5,13.5l2.5,3.01L14.5,12l4.5,6H5l3.5,-4.5z" />

</vector>
